\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Qualitative explanation disagreement on RSNA lumbar spine MRI. For a fixed fold-0 sample and target prediction, post-hoc explanation methods produce visibly different saliency patterns across DenseNet-121 and ViT-Small. Attention Rollout is Transformer-native and is therefore not applicable to CNN rows. The gallery motivates the benchmark question studied in this paper: whether such disagreement is method noise alone or whether it is systematically shaped by model architecture.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gallery}{{1}{2}{Qualitative explanation disagreement on RSNA lumbar spine MRI. For a fixed fold-0 sample and target prediction, post-hoc explanation methods produce visibly different saliency patterns across DenseNet-121 and ViT-Small. Attention Rollout is Transformer-native and is therefore not applicable to CNN rows. The gallery motivates the benchmark question studied in this paper: whether such disagreement is method noise alone or whether it is systematically shaped by model architecture}{figure.1}{}}
